from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "1. The Question, the Method and the Five Settings"),
("p", "Claim: the question of healing and the Body is answered by reading each passage in its own setting and asking three things of it: who is speaking, to whom, and for what purpose. Healing appears in Moses’ law, in David’s psalm, in the Lord’s ministry in Galilee, in the commission of the Twelve, in the book of Acts, in Paul’s letters and in the letter of James. The passages do not all say the same thing, and a reader who treats them as one statement will miss what each says."),
("sub", "1.1 The question"),
("p", "The question is not whether God heals. The Scriptures answer that without hesitation: “I kill, and I make alive; I wound, and I heal” (Deuteronomy 32:39). The question is whether any passage addressed to the Body of Christ gives healing as a gift, as an authority or as a promise, and what the letters addressed to the Body do with sickness when they meet it. These are questions about the Body’s own letters, and they are the ones this volume answers."),
("sub", "1.2 The five settings"),
("p", "The volume reads healing in five settings, and each has its own chapter or chapters. The first is Israel’s Scriptures, where the LORD promises health to a people under covenant and heals a king and a captain (chapter 3). The second is the Lord’s ministry in the Gospels, where His healings are named as the fulfilment of a prophecy (chapter 4). The third is the commission of the Twelve and the Seventy and the book of Acts as far as the eighth chapter (chapter 5). The fourth is Acts 9 to 28 and the gift of healing at Corinth (chapters 6 and 7). The fifth is the letters, those of James and Peter, addressed to scattered Israel, and those of Paul, addressed to the Body (chapters 8 to 11)."),
("p", "The settings are not arranged to prove a conclusion. They are the order in which the text places them, and the order matters. The healings of the Gospels come before the cross and are done in the land of Israel to Israelites. The healings of Acts 1 to 8 are done by the Twelve in Jerusalem and Judaea and Samaria. The healings of Acts 9 to 28 are done in a period the volume calls the overlap, in which Israel’s Prophecy Program was still operative and the Mystery Program had begun. The letters of Paul are written within that period and after it."),
("sub", "1.3 The method"),
("p", "The method has four parts. Each passage is quoted from the King James Version and checked word for word. Each is read in the chapter or the book in which it stands, with the verses around it. Each is asked who speaks, who hears and what purpose the passage names. And the volume says what a passage leaves unsaid, because an argument from silence is a different thing from an argument from a statement, and the reader is owed the difference."),
("p", "The volume rests its conclusion on three kinds of evidence. The first is what passages state: that the Lord sent the Twelve to the house of Israel, that James writes to the twelve tribes, that Paul prayed thrice about his thorn and was answered with grace. The second is what the record shows: that the last healing Acts records is on Malta, in the last chapter, and that no healing is recorded after it. The third is what the letters do not contain: no letter addressed to the Body tells anyone to heal, to seek healing as a gift, or to expect it as a promise. The first two are plain. The third is a silence, and the volume says so where it relies on it."),
("sub", "1.4 What the volume does not decide"),
("p", "The volume does not say how God heals, whether He heals today, whether a given recovery is the work of medicine or of mercy, or whether a given modern account is true. It says what the passages say healing is for and when the program in which it served ends. It leaves God’s power where the Scriptures leave it, with God."),
("tbl", "The five settings and the passages read in each.", [
    ("Exodus 15:26", T("Exod 15:26"), "The LORD speaks to Israel under covenant: obedience and the promise of health."),
    ("Matthew 10:5–8", T("Matt 10:5-8"), "The Twelve are sent to Israel and told to heal."),
    ("Acts 28:8, 9", T("Acts 28:8, 9"), "The last healing the record gives: Paul on Malta."),
    ("1 Corinthians 12:28", T("1 Cor 12:28"), "Gifts of healings among the gifts God has set in the church."),
    ("James 1:1", T("Jas 1:1"), "James writes to the twelve tribes."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Healing is read in five settings, in the order the text places them.",
    "Each passage is asked who speaks, who hears and what purpose it names.",
    "The volume says what each passage leaves unsaid.",
    "The volume makes no statement about God’s power and does not judge any modern account.",
  ]),
]
